SECTION A — CONDITIONALS AND DECISION LOGIC
============================================================

Q01. Electricity Bill Calculator
Input units consumed. Calculate the bill using these slabs:
- First 100 units: Rs. 5 per unit
- Next 100 units: Rs. 7 per unit
- Above 200 units: Rs. 10 per unit
Calculate progressively, not by charging every unit at the highest slab. Handle zero and
negative input sensibly.

Q02. Shopping Discount Calculator
Calculate discount based on purchase amount:
- Below Rs. 1,000: no discount
- Rs. 1,000 to Rs. 2,999.99: 5%
- Rs. 3,000 to Rs. 4,999.99: 10%
- Rs. 5,000 and above: 15%
Display original amount, discount amount, and final amount. Test exact boundaries.

Q03. ATM Withdrawal Validator
Given account balance and withdrawal amount, reject invalid amounts, amounts not divisible
by 100, insufficient balance, and withdrawals that would leave the balance below Rs. 500.
Otherwise show the remaining balance.

Q04. Grade and Result Analyzer
Accept marks for five subjects. Reject marks outside 0–100. Calculate total and average,
determine pass/fail based on a minimum 40 in every subject, and assign a grade using
clearly documented grade boundaries.

Q05. Leap Year Checker
Determine whether a year is a leap year. Test century years and years divisible by 400.

Q06. Triangle Classifier
Given three side lengths, first determine whether they form a valid triangle. If valid,
classify it as equilateral, isosceles, or scalene.

Q07. Login Attempt System
Store a sample username and password in variables. Allow at most three attempts. Give
useful messages for incorrect username, incorrect password, and successful login.
Do not reveal the real password in error messages.

Q08. Date Validator
Given day, month, and year, determine whether the date exists. Account for different month
lengths and leap years.

============================================================
SECTION B — LOOPS AND COUNTERS
============================================================

Q09. Sum from 1 to N
Ask for a positive integer N and calculate the sum from 1 through N using a loop.
Do not use the arithmetic-series formula.

Q10. Multiplication Table
Print the multiplication table for a number from 1 through 12. Format the output clearly.

Q11. Count Even and Odd Numbers
For all integers from 1 through N, count how many are even and how many are odd.
Validate N before starting.

Q12. Factorial Calculator
Calculate N! using a loop. Handle 0! correctly and reject negative or invalid input.
Consider what happens when N is very large.

Q13. Reverse a Number
Reverse an integer mathematically using %, division, and Math.floor(). Do not convert
the number to a string and do not use reverse() or split().

Q14. Count Digits
Count the digits in an integer without converting it to a string. Decide how your program
will handle zero and negative numbers.

Q15. Sum of Digits
Calculate the sum of the digits of an integer using a loop and arithmetic operations.

Q16. Count Even and Odd Digits
For an input integer, count its even digits and odd digits. Example: 58321 contains two
even digits (8 and 2) and three odd digits (5, 3, and 1).

Q17. Product of Digits
Calculate the product of all digits in an integer. Think carefully about how zero digits
affect the result.

Q18. Palindrome Number
Determine whether an integer reads the same forward and backward. Solve without converting
it to a string.

Q19. Prime Number Checker
Determine whether a number is prime. Reject values below 2 and avoid unnecessary checks.

Q20. Print Prime Numbers
Print all prime numbers between 1 and N. Keep the prime-checking logic understandable.

Q21. Fibonacci Sequence
Print the first N terms of the Fibonacci sequence. Test N values of 0, 1, and 2.

Q22. Greatest Common Divisor
Find the GCD of two integers using a loop. Try the Euclidean algorithm and explain why it
works in your README.

Q23. Least Common Multiple
Find the LCM of two integers. Handle zero inputs and avoid needless repeated checks where
possible.

Q24. Armstrong Number
Check whether a number equals the sum of its digits each raised to the power of the number
of digits. Test more than just three-digit examples.

Q25. Largest Digit
Find the largest digit in an integer without converting it to a string and without using
Math.max().

Q26. Second-Largest Distinct Number
Find the second-largest distinct value in an array without using sort(), sorted(), max(),
or min(). Define what should happen if no second-largest distinct value exists.

